{
	title: 'Startup cost follows open tabs, not sessions on disk',
	spec: 'Every startup that becomes host recovers unfinished turns and queued messages in open tabs and in non-tab sessions marked busy. Its cost grows with open tabs and the durable busy list, never with every session on disk: it reads no metadata or history of unrelated closed sessions, and for idle tabs it reads marks.ason instead of history tails. Closing a tab pauses its turn before removing it; a closed tab never resumes work unseen after restart. Queued messages are found from the durable inbox marks even if far back in history. Budget: with 2,000 closed sessions and 100 open idle tabs on disk, recovery finishes in under 10 ms on an M1-class machine; a test measures files and bytes read as well as time. Tests cover a queued message written 1 MB before the end of history and a turn closed mid-stream staying paused after restart.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Built: src/host/busy.ts keeps state/busy.ason, the ids of sessions that may hold work. history.append adds the session before writing a user, continue or inbox record (saved at once, so a crash leaves an extra entry, never a missing one) and drops it after a turn_end when marks.inbox is empty; recover drops idle ones it checks. recover walks tabs.ason open ids plus busy ids, decides from marks alone (turns.leftWork: the record at marks.turn is no turn_end, or a queued inbox after a completed turn) and reads the whole history only for sessions with work.',
		"Sessions made with the 'create' command (most host tests) are not tabs: without the busy list recovery would never see them. Anything new that writes work into history must go through history.append, or it bypasses the busy list.",
		'history.tail (the 64 KiB guess) is gone; history.unfinished reads marks.turn and one line. sessions.list still reads every metadata file; nothing on the startup path may call it.',
		'The 10 ms budget test takes the best of three cold runs (host reset between) so a loaded machine does not fail it; it measured about 5 ms with the machine under load 10. The startup.test.ts timing test also fails under such load regardless of this task.',
		'The older recovery test expected closed running tabs to continue; task 8v reversed that rule. Recovery still handles non-tab busy sessions and queued work.'
	],
